hello everyone this is  a research scholar from department of  bioinformatics pondicherry university  so today i am going to present  on oncogenic and non-oncogenic class  prediction for human papillomavirus  using genomic data and machine learning  approaches  so human papillomavirus is a double  standard dna virus which belongs to  papillomavidic family and it used to  cause warts and  carcinomas till that more than 220 types  of human papillomavirus has been  discovered and some of them are  asymptomatic and some of them used to  cause  carcinomas based on their genotypes they  can be little as little as they can  cause penile cancer head and neck cancer  and other energetical cancer  and some of them are asymptomatic  however  hpv and cervical cancer cervical cancer  relationship is well studied  and it is well established  as the  with advancement in technology new hpv  types are adding every year to the  database  and as the  computational power is also increasing  such as  the methods such as machine learning  models are being used in biological  senses  it is important to analyze these data to  find a relevant pattern in the data  using machine learning models so  in past few years genomic data and codon  uses information is being used  with machine learning models to annotate  the genomes  so here what we have done is  we have used rsco information to  categorize these two classes to classify  these two classes so you can see this is  known carcinogenic and this is  carcinogenic class  so here we have used six core genes from  hpv genome which are present across the  mammalian genome  so e1 and a2 which are involved in  replication and transcription and  these e6 and e7 are present in they are  oncoprotein and two of them l and l two  are capsid proteins so what we have done  is we have taken six of these protein we  have constructed an rsu table best  relative codon synonymous codon usage  table which will tell you the codon  biases so based on the rsc value more  than 1.6 we have constructed a table and  from there we have summed up based on  the wobble nucleotide at the third  position so uh if  a  on third position if a  adenine is there so all that and  all the codons which are having adenine  we have summed up the rsc value so this  is how we are constructed for four  bubble nucleotides for each gene so we  got 24 features  uh in a feature table for six of the  genes and we have fed this two machine  learning model which was able to  classify the carcinogenic and  non-carcinogenic class of the  hpv for this we have uh  used uh linear and  non-linear model combination such as  linear regression  and linear discriminant analysis    abs and svm  so we have find out based on training  and testing data we have found out svm  and logistic regression performed well  and we have a hypertune the svm model  and we find out that linear kernel with  scale gamma performed based on our test  data set  and logistic regression also performed  almost same as you can see  at the end uh we found out with svm the  model accuracy on five rows five fold  cross validation it was 98 percent 98.4  percent on trending data set and 98  percent on test data set the mcc value  on test data set was 90.91 which is 91  the specificity and sensitivity which  are the  important measures in machine learning  model  accuracy  evaluation so we are having specificity  100 percent and sensitivity 98 percent  and balanced accuracy on our imbalance  test data set was 99  and as you can see  in our  in our confusion matrix we are having  six of the carcinogenic sample  classified correctly out of 57 56  non-carcinogenic classified correctly  only one non-carcinogenic is classified  as carcinoma  so in our initial  data set we were having 18 carcinogenic  and 129 non-carcinogenic  at the end we created  a balanced data set by oversampling the  minor class which is carcinogenic so 129  129 a balanced data set we have created  and we tested on our test data set  so what we concluded out of that is  linear models such as svm  with linear kernel can be used such as  logistic regression linear models can be  also used for hpv class prediction  and  genomic composition information such as  codon usage information can be  integrated into these models to classify  newly discovered hpv phenotype and these  models can be helpful for the clinician  in early diagnosis of novel hpv  so we have got the roc well do  on test data set is almost 100  so this  so we would like to  thank uh  dvd  for providing a  financial assistance in the form of  liberty build philosophy  thank you everyone  you
 

